Accumulate scalar quantities over rectangles and volumes (@math-fields-regions: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Distinguish-scalar-path-accumulation-from-oriented-circulation.lingua#r_00D6A7AD4898E4E6881370A983") { r_00EBDB50A31BF3955DD0E3A3B7
Begin with [Define the integral and distinguish net change from total magnitude](Define-the-integral-and-distinguish-net-change-from-total-magnitude.lingua#r_0AEC763D73DF3124CDBB2E86D1) and [Compose local maps and derive change along a path](Compose-local-maps-and-derive-change-along-a-path.lingua#r_001110BBF317180AC63FAA5412). A scalar field assigns one number to each point of a stated domain. If the number is amount per area, multiplying by a small area approximates an amount; if it is amount per volume, use volume. Signed quantities can cancel. The geometrical measure of a region is nonnegative even when the field is negative. Coordinates here are Cartesian in a fixed Euclidean space; a different coordinate system will require its measure factor.

For a rectangle R=[a,b]×[c,d] with increasing bounds, subdivide each interval and choose any point in each subrectangle. Form Σ f(tag) Δx Δy. Define ∬_R f dA=I when every sufficiently fine tagged product partition has sum within any requested ε of I. The mesh is the largest cell diameter, not just the number of cells. Triple integrals over boxes use Δx Δy Δz. This extends the existing one-dimensional definition without treating dx as a finite number to cancel.

For continuous f on a compact rectangle, uniform continuity controls the largest oscillation ω(δ) on a cell of diameter at most δ. The difference between upper and lower sums is at most area(R)ω(δ), which tends to zero. Refinement makes lower sums increase and upper sums decrease; real completeness gives their common value. Every tagged sum lies between its bounds. This is the existence argument under those hypotheses. Additivity and linearity follow from finite sums and limits. For nonrectangular bounded regions whose boundary has Jordan area zero, use enclosing rectangles and the indicator of the region; the integrability extension and general change-of-variables theorem are accepted analysis results here, not proved by a drawing.

Repeated integration is justified for these continuous rectangle cases. For each fixed y, integrate in x. Uniform continuity bounds the error of an x-sum uniformly in y by (b−a)ω(δ); integrating that bound in y makes the error at most area(R)ω(δ). The resulting finite sums converge to the same double integral. Reversing the roles gives the other order. Apply the argument once more to continuous functions on boxes. For general singular/unbounded functions, changing the order needs new hypotheses.

On [0,2]×[−1,1], f=1+x+2y has integral 8: the constant contributes 4, x contributes 4, and the odd y term contributes zero. The answer does not say f is everywhere positive. On [0,2]×[−1,1]×[0,3], a constant density 5 integrates to 60. Retain the density's units in each answer; an integral of density need not be geometrical volume.

**Read and work:** [Calculus and differential-equation preparation — Strang and Herman, OpenStax](../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-OpenStax.lingua#r_027WKVM5NG5X84M86TSVRA77J3), Volume 3 section 5.1, its definition and continuous-case iterated integrals; then work the corresponding box extension in 5.4 as assigned further reading. Compare the explicit mesh/continuity argument here with a regular grid illustration. Integrate 2−x+z on the box above by two orders, obtaining 30, and explain why one zero integral cannot prove the integrand vanishes.

**Continue:** [Distinguish scalar path accumulation from oriented circulation](Distinguish-scalar-path-accumulation-from-oriented-circulation.lingua#r_00D6A7AD4898E4E6881370A983).

